{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>עַד מָתַי יִהְיֶה</b> etc. It is not that they agreed to send Israel off permanently, for the prophecy that HaShem said to Moshe — ״הִכְבַּדְתִּי״ etc. — would contradict this. Rather, they wanted them to go in a manner such that they would surely return. And therefore you will find that when Moshe said ״בִּנְעָרֵינוּ וּבִזְקֵנֵינוּ״ etc. and Pharaoh drove them out, they did not continue to speak further about this matter. Also, their own mouths testified against them, for they did not believe in the order of events — that the great, mighty, and awesome Elohim of the Hebrews had stretched forth His hand against them to send Israel off; rather, they remained obstinate against them, and there is no greater denial of the truth of the matter than this." "<b>הֲטֶרֶם תֵּדַע.</b> Meaning: do you wish to know that Egypt is destroyed before you send them? Therefore send them off before this condition of Egypt's destruction is reached." "<b>And I saw</b> to give attention to what it was that their minds agreed to regarding the destruction of Egypt afterward, and yet they remained silent and went their way. And to understand the matter, let us apply our understanding in investigating what was the mindset of Pharaoh and his servants from the beginning of the affair. Is there any utter fool in the world who would suffer all those evils and troubles and place himself in mortal danger — and on top of that, his god has been struck, his livestock have perished, the produce of the earth has been destroyed, and they had nearly perished altogether, as they themselves said: ״הֲטֶרֶם תֵּדַע כִּי אָבְדָה מִצְרָיִם״? And by simple reasoning one cannot believe that he was so foolish; and even though HaShem hardened his spirit, with all this there must have been some rationale through which his foolishness would be revealed. And the difficulty grows greater when you see that HaShem asked of him nothing but a three-day journey, and even though we wrote there (Exodus 3:18) that HaShem did not state this particular detail except to Israel, with all that, in all his missions he said nothing but (Exodus 5:1) ״וְיָחֹגּוּ לִי בַּמִּדְבָּר״, ״וְיַעַבְדֻנִי״ (Exodus 5:3) — this would indicate that HaShem did not command permanently." "And I understand a thing: that it was this — the manner of the mission that HaShem sent to send Israel to celebrate in the wilderness, that the intention is to return afterward — that misled Pharaoh and all his people. For it was clear to Pharaoh and his people that He was not asking permanently, and from this the error fell, for the matter has two sides: the first, that the word of HaShem is true, that He desires nothing but a three-day journey; and the second, that the words came by way of cunning and His intent is only to take them out permanently. And according to this, the words testify that, as it were, He lacks the power to take them out permanently — far be it — from which you learn that He has, Heaven forbid, some constraint upon His will. And therefore Pharaoh and his servants, on the side of the absence of core faith in them, reckoned with the second side, and therefore they stiffened their necks and refused to accept the word of HaShem, for they said He lacks the power, Heaven forbid, to compel a great compulsion. And this for you is the wondrous sign that nakedness arouses. And therefore they hardened their hearts. But afterward, when his servants saw that seven plagues had already passed over them and the warning of the eighth plague of the locusts had come, they gave heed to consider the first side — for the truth is that the intent of the blessed Elohim is that He does not desire to take them out permanently. And this is their saying <b>עַד מָתַי יִהְיֶה</b> etc. <b>שַׁלַּח אֶת הָאֲנָשִׁים וְיַעַבְדוּ</b> — meaning, that the intent of HaShem is only regarding this particular detail; and the reason by which we compelled the matter is that we recognized that He is omnipotent, and if His intent had been that they go permanently, He would have said so explicitly and would have destroyed every resister and refuser of His words. And they said <b>הֲטֶרֶם תֵּדַע כִּי אָבְדָה מִצְרָיִם</b> by the plagues that He struck — from which you learn that He is able to do all that He desires. And according to this they established two conclusions: the first, that Israel would not go permanently; and they justified this from the wonders of the plagues — for if HaShem had desired to take them out permanently, no one could save from His hand." "<b>And you will find</b> that the wicked Pharaoh acted cunningly and answered them with deceit: ״וַיּוּשַׁב אֶת מֹשֶׁה״ etc., ״וַיֹּאמֶר לָהֶם מִי וָמִי הַהוֹלְכִים״, and they answered him ״בִּנְעָרֵינוּ וּבִזְקֵנֵינוּ נֵלֵךְ״, and he said to them ״רָעָה נֶגֶד״ etc. ״לְכוּ נָא הַגְּבָרִים״ etc. ״כִּי אוֹתָהּ אַתֶּם מְבַקְשִׁים, וַיְגָרֶשׁ אוֹתָם״ etc. And from the midst of the events, the mindset of Pharaoh's servants was hidden — for it was not as they had thought, that their going was only temporary; for from the affair it is proven that there is cunning in the matter, and their going is a going for eternity. And according to this they reverted as well to the first suspicion, for this for you is the sign that He is not omnipotent as mentioned above; and because of this they did not continue to speak further as they had in their first words."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:26:36.999432616Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}